    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>According to the traditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion, Ife was the first city, built on the earth that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> cast on the primordial ocean, and the kingdoms of Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Egba, Ijesha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and others were founded by his descendants.</li><li>The city was founded between 1000 and 500 BCE, was an important West African emporium by 900 CE, and reached a classical period in the 1100s in which its artists made naturalistic sculpture in terracotta, stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy.</li><li>Production of that art declined after about 1400 as political and economic power shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, but Ife remained the seat of the Ọọ̀ni and, in tradition, the city of 401 deities.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ife glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> have been found at sites as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and Igbo Olokun may be one of the earliest glass-making workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

