    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ifẹ̀ was founded sometime between 1000 BCE and 500 BCE. The city lies in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, on undulating land with deep clay soils on the upper slopes and sandy soils on the lower ones. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> the name is explained from the word ifẹ̀, which means expansion, so that Ilé-Ifẹ̀ is read as the land of expansion or, with ilé as house or home, the home of expansion.</p><p>The Yoruba religion gives the city a place in the making of the world. In the traditions of that religion Ilé-Ifẹ̀ was founded by the order of the Supreme Deity Olódùmarè, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Ọbatala</a>. In the account told of the creation, Olódùmarè ordered Obatala to create the earth, but on the way he drank too much palm wine and became drunk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> took the items of creation from him, descended from the abode of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a> on a chain and cast a handful of earth on the primordial ocean. The earth rose as a mound called Oke Ora, and a five-toed cockerel set on it scattered the earth around, making the land on which the first city would be built. Oduduwa planted a palm nut, from which sprang a tree with sixteen branches, a symbol of the 16 clans of the early Ife confederation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 900 CE Ife had become an important West African emporium that produced sophisticated art forms, and by the 1100s it had entered a classical period and become the capital of a significant regional empire. It was a settlement of substantial size between the 12th and 14th centuries, with houses that had pavements of potsherds.</p><p>Its sculpture in bronze, stone and terracotta reached a peak between 1200 and 1400. Around 1300 the artists of Ife developed a refined and naturalistic tradition in terracotta, stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy, many of the works apparently made under the patronage of King Obalufon II, who is identified today as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> patron deity of brass casting, weaving and regalia. Ife was also famous for its glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, found at sites as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and glass bead making at Igbo Olokun was at its height between the 11th and 15th centuries. After the great period of its art, production declined as political and economic power shifted to the nearby kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, which, like the Yoruba kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, grew into a major empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oranmiyan.jpg" alt="Oranmiyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opa_Oranmiyan_at_Ile-Ife_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abby's Lens, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition holds that Ilé-Ifẹ̀ fell into the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Ọbatala</a>, and that this created enmity between the two. Oduduwa created a dynasty there. The first Ọọ̀ni of Ifẹ̀ was a descendant of Oduduwa, who is counted as the 401st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a>, and the sons and daughters of the dynasty became rulers of many other kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>.</p><p>The same tradition names the kingdoms that Oduduwa's sons, daughters and grandchildren founded: Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ijesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, among others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a>, Oduduwa's last born or, in another telling, his grandson, served as a minister and overseer of the nascent Edo kingdom. When he returned to Ile Ife he left behind a son, Eweka, born to an indigenous princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, and Eweka became the first widely accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of the second Edo dynasty. Oranmiyan later migrated north-westwards to found Oyo, which grew into an empire that at its height stretched from the western banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> to the eastern banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>, until its collapse in the mid 19th century.</p><p>The title of Ọọ̀ni is held to have passed to every king of Ife after Oduduwa. The 49th Ọọ̀ni was Adesoji Aderemi and the 50th Okunade Sijuwade, who died in 2015.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ife is called the city of 401 deities, the irumole or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orishas</a>. Traditional worshippers are said to celebrate a festival of one of them on every day of the year. The festivals often last more than a day and combine the activities of priests in the palace with dramatic performances elsewhere in the kingdom. Historically the king appeared in public only at the annual Olojo festival, which celebrates the new dawn. Other major festivals are the Itapa festival for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Obatala</a>, the Edi festival for Moremi Ajasoro and the Ugbo festival with its Igare masqueraders. The rivalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> and Obatala is re-enacted by the followers of the two deities at the Itapa festival, which marks the new year.</p><p>The city had shrines and groves. Igbo Olokun, a forested sacred grove, housed shrines of the goddess Olokun and had glass makers who worked there. The Oduduwa shrine and grove is that of the progenitor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, and the Agbonniregun temple is the grove of Ọrunmila, the Orisha of wisdom, knowledge and divination. Artists often drew kings and gods with large heads, because they believed that ase, the inner power of a person, was held in the head. Excavations at Iyekere found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, pottery and the remains of iron smelting, which was carried out in the Ife region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition records the conflict that followed the making of the world. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> took the work of creation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Obatala</a>, the usurpation gave rise to a lasting rivalry between the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a>, which their followers still re-enact.</p><p>The decline of Ife's sculpture is the one change in the city's fortunes that is dated, though only loosely. After the period between 1200 and 1400, the production of that art declined as political and economic power shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, which grew into a major empire.</p><p>The glass-making of the Yoruba is said to have persisted into the seventeenth century in Osun Grove, and iron smelting in the Ife region has been placed, with caution, in the Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> before colonial rule. Colonisation by the British also affected the Ọọ̀ni: after it, no holder of the title had the international standing that the Ọọ̀ni acquired following the formation of the Yoruba Orisha Congress in 1986.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo.jpg" alt="Obafemi Awolowo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo">Obafemi Awolowo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obafemi_Awolowo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eliot Elisofon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ife is known worldwide for its ancient and naturalistic sculptures in bronze, stone and terracotta, which date to between 1200 and 1400 CE and are significant examples of naturalism in pre-colonial African art. They are distinguished by their variations in regalia, facial marking patterns and body proportions, and they include the life-size <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mask honouring Obalufon II, who is said to have invented bronze casting. The glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of Igbo Olokun are evidence of a glass industry that, by composition, was largely independent of glass-making traditions documented farther afield and took part in regional trade networks.</p><p>The city remains the holy city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, and the Ọọ̀ni is regarded as the chief priest and custodian of that city. The old palace was built of enamelled bricks and decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> tiles and ornaments. Ife is today a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, about 218 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, with two local government areas. It has a regional agricultural centre and is the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo">Obafemi Awolowo</a> University, formerly the University of Ife.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ifẹ̀ is an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, founded between 1000 and 500 BCE and held by Yoruba tradition to be the first city. It was a regional capital and a centre of naturalistic sculpture and glass-making, and its Ọọ̀ni remains the chief priest of the Yoruba.</em></p>

